  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 205 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\n(Friday Night - Saturday Night)\n\nThe focus for both Friday and Saturday nights is on the potential \nfor the first freeze this fall in the region. The coldest night for \nmost locations is expected to be Friday night based on the timing of \nthe surface high, though portions of southeast Missouri in favored \nriver valleys may be as cold, if not slightly colder, Saturday \nnight. Widespread low 30s are forecast across the region Friday \nnight, with the exception of the urban heat island of metro St. \nLouis (mid to upper 30s). A few spots may drop into the upper 20s, \nmainly in central/northeast Missouri as well as west-central \nIllinois. This is where the REFS probabilities for subfreezing low \ntemperatures are highest (60-70%). The surface high Saturday night \nis retreating slowly across southeast Missouri and southwest \nIllinois. Lows in the low 30s are forecast in these locations, with \nmid 30s to the northwest due to increasing southwesterly surface \nflow. \n\n\n(Sunday - Next Thursday)\n\nThere remains high confidence in a dry weather pattern with \nmoderating temperatures late this weekend into early next week. By \nMonday, high temperatures are expected to be back well into the 60s, \nwith lows in the 30s/40s. Forecast uncertainty increases mid/late \nweek with respect to the strength and location of a mid/upper level \ntrough moving across the Great Lakes/northeast. There is quite a bit \nof spread with this feature, with two of the four clusters of the \n500-hPa height pattern from the LREF showing a deeper trough closer \nto the Great Lakes, while two other clusters are weaker and farther \nto the east. While mostly dry weather is likely to continue in \neither scenario, a stronger trough across the Great Lakes would have \nthe potential to send us the coldest air so far this season. The \ntemperature spread on the NBM expands considerably between Tuesday \nand next Thursday, with differences between the 25th/75th \npercentiles over 10 degrees for both highs and lows in some \nlocations.",
